The code given above is already outputting assert statements inside each method. However, I have prepared a Python script which can help you generate csharp code which contains assert statements for the comments that start with "@@@" (if comment does not have the @ and @ in it). You can replace the generated code in the existing test file.

```python
import re

def extract_asserts(code):
    regex = re.compile(r'// @@+\s*(.*)\n')
    matches = re.findall(regex, code)
    return matches

code = """
...
"""

asserts = extract_asserts(code)

new_code = code.replace("...", "\n".join(["\tDebug.Assert({});\n".format(ass) for ass in asserts]))

print(new_code)
```

This script works by using a regular expression to find all of the lines that start with "@@@" and then formatting them to be a Debug.Assert call. The "..." placeholder in the original code is replaced with the results of this operation.--------------------This problem can be solved using a Python script that utilizes the `re` package to parse the comment lines and find the ones starting with "@@@". This pattern will be used to find the respective assertion statement lines and then those can be extracted and converted into debug.Assert statements. 

Here's a basic python script that will do the job:

```python
import re
import os

# file path 
path = "Your/File/Path/File.cs"

# Read file
with open(path, 'r') as file:
    data = file.read().splitlines()

assert_lines = [re.sub('^.*?@', 'Debug.Assert(', line) + ');' for line in data if line.strip().startswith('// @@@')]
print("\n".join(assert_lines))
```

Adjust your file path where "Your/File/Path/File.cs" is the path to your c# file. Please note that this script assumes all assert lines will start with "// @@@" and that they will be the only comment lines with assert statements. 

You can run this script after modifying the path. It replaces the comment lines beginning with "// @@@" to include the debug.Assert() and prints the resulting lines.--------------------Here is a program that could output the `Debug.Assert(...)` statements corresponding to the comments that start with "@@@". However, the implementation you ask for is not feasible, because I can not read the logic and assumptions made in this code.

Here is a basic program that could be used to create tests from comments but does not account for the code logic:

```cs
using System;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        string code = @"
            // @@ @@@ some comment
            Debug.Assert(true);

            // @@ another comment

            Debug.Assert(false);
        ";

        var comments = Regex.Matches(code, "@@ .*");

        foreach (Match comment in comments)
        {
            var trimmed = comment.Value.Trim().TrimStart('@').Trim();
            Console.WriteLine($"Debug.Assert({trimmed});");
        }
    }
}
```

Keep in mind, the logic of C# methods and conditions are not straightforward to process and translate into 'Assert' methods, because it involves complex conditions and many possible scenarios.

